# Integral with integral inverse over K[j] implies order zero

Theorem statement: `AlgebraicCurve.Place.ord_eq_zero_of_isIntegral_adjoin`
(`cf819755-b119-5538-81c0-fd5d8764e198`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `c4de5ba7-527e-5d01-8a09-a46cff0a51c2`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $K$ and $F$ be fields with $F$ a $K$-algebra, and let $v$ be a place of $F$ over $K$ in the
project's sense: a valuation subring $v.\mathrm{toValuationSubring}$ of $F$ that contains the image
of $K$ under the structure map, is not all of $F$, and is a principal ideal ring. Let $j, x \in F$,
suppose $j$ lies in $v.\mathrm{toValuationSubring}$, and suppose that both $x$ and $x^{-1}$ are
integral over the $K$-subalgebra $\mathrm{Algebra.adjoin}\ K\ \{j\}$ of $F$ generated by $j$, i.e.
each is a root of a monic polynomial with coefficients in $K[j]$. Then $v.\mathrm{ord}\ x = 0$,
where $v.\mathrm{ord}$ is minus the logarithm of the value of the adic valuation attached to $v$
(the $\mathbb{Z}^{m0}$-valued valuation of the height-one prime of $v.\mathrm{toValuationSubring}$),
so that $v.\mathrm{ord}$ is the normalised order of vanishing at $v$, with the value $0$ at $x = 0$
by Lean's inversion convention $0^{-1} = 0$ being consistent with the hypotheses.

The statement records that an element of $F$ which, together with its inverse, is integral over
$K[j]$ is a unit at every place where $j$ is integral, hence has order zero there. It is used in the
modular-curve part of the development, for instance in locating the support of divisors of modular
units at the cusps and in the height and cuspidal-class computations that cite it.

